-- infra/e2e/seed/50-phase3-batch1-miniapp-fixture.sql.tmpl
--
-- Deterministic tenant-DB fixture for infra/e2e/api-parity.mjs (Phase 3
-- batch 1 — mig-infra's JSON API parity harness covering the six ported
-- /api/miniapp/** endpoints x 16 actions: resolve-line-account,
-- points-history:history, shop-products:{products,product_detail,categories},
-- health-profile:get, member:{check,get_card,register,update_profile},
-- rewards:{list,redeem,my_redemptions}, wishlist:{list,toggle,remove}).
--
-- Applied by api-parity.mjs AFTER database/migration_2026-05-25_tenant_template.sql
-- (same TENANT_TEMPLATE parity.mjs already applies) and AFTER
-- 45-phase3-batch1-plan-and-tenant.sql.tmpl, with `USE __APP_DB_NAME__;`
-- prefixed by the harness (same convention as
-- 30-phase2-batch1-fixture.sql.tmpl / 40-phase2-batch2-fixture.sql.tmpl).
--
-- SCHEMA-DDL NOTE (corrects a premise in the originating brief): the brief
-- asked this file to ALSO carry verbatim CREATE TABLE DDL for
-- `user_wishlist` (api/wishlist.php) and `user_health_profiles` /
-- `user_drug_allergies` / `user_current_medications` (api/health-profile.php)
-- on the claim those 4 tables are "NOT in database/install_complete_latest.sql
-- or the tenant template migration". Verified false by reading both files: all
-- 4 tables already exist, with IDENTICAL column definitions to the PHP
-- runtime auto-create blocks, in database/migration_2026-05-25_tenant_template.sql
-- (user_wishlist at its line ~298, user_current_medications/user_drug_allergies/
-- user_health_profiles at its lines ~2696-2743) — the same template file
-- TENANT_TEMPLATE already applies before this fixture runs. Duplicating
-- `CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS` here would be a harmless no-op (IF NOT EXISTS)
-- but is dead weight given the tables are already guaranteed present; this
-- file therefore seeds DATA ONLY. Flagged here (not silently dropped) per
-- this batch's own instruction to surface brief/reality mismatches rather
-- than silently "fixing" them.
--
-- IDENTITY STRATEGY FOR WRITE ACTIONS (member:check, member:register,
-- member:update_profile, rewards:redeem, wishlist:toggle, wishlist:remove):
-- each gets TWO separately-seeded, structurally-identical identities —
-- suffixed `-php` / `-next` — one exercised against the PHP stack, one
-- against the Next stack, so neither run consumes state the other run's
-- assertions depend on (api-parity.mjs's own header comment documents this
-- same strategy — see that file for why "two seeded users" was chosen over
-- a reset-between-runs step: it lets both calls happen in either order
-- without a teardown step between them, and keeps the DB-row-shape
-- comparison meaningful — querying the PHP-identity's resulting row against
-- the Next-identity's resulting row directly, modulo the allowlist).
--
-- All row IDs below are EXPLICIT (not left to AUTO_INCREMENT) so
-- infra/e2e/lib/api-extract.mjs's request builders can reference them as
-- plain constants — this is a fresh, ephemeral, single-tenant DB (docker
-- compose down -v every run), so explicit PK values never collide with
-- anything the tenant template itself seeds (it seeds none of these tables).
--
-- All `created_at`/`updated_at`/`registered_at`/`approved_at` values below
-- are explicit, fixed, past timestamps — deliberately NOT relying on
-- CURRENT_TIMESTAMP defaults — so the ONLY genuinely-nondeterministic values
-- the harness must allowlist are the ones a request handler itself
-- generates at request time (redemption_code, newly auto-registered
-- member_id/user id, member:register's freshly-INSERTed id, health-profile's
-- write-side timestamps are irrelevant here since `get` is read-only and
-- every row below is pre-seeded, never freshly INSERTed by the GET itself).

